        <obo:IAO_0000115>An atomic nucleus with 73 protons and 108 neutrons </obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>Ta-181 ion;Ta-181 nucleus;Ta-181 </obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>Tantalum-181 nucleus</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005052 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005052">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010012"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An atomic nucleus with 79 protons and 118 neutrons </obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>Au-197 ion;Au-197 nucleus;Au-197 </obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>Gold-197 nucleus</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005053 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005053">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010012"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An atomic nucleus with 7 protons and 7 neutrons </obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>N-14 ion;N-14 nucleus;N-14 </obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>Nitrogen-14 nucleus</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005054 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005054">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010012"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An atomic nucleus with 8 protons and 8 neutrons </obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>O-16 ion;O-16 nucleus;O-16 </obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>Oxygen-16 nucleus</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005055 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005055">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010012"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An atomic nucleus with 18 protons and 22 neutrons </obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>Ar-40  ion;Ar-40  nucleus;Ar-40  </obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>Argon-40  nucleus</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005056 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005056">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010012"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An atomic nucleus with 22 protons and 26 neutrons </obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>Ti-48  ion;Ti-48  nucleus;Ti-48  </obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>Titanium-48  nucleus</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005057 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005057">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010012"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An atomic nucleus with 36 protons and 48 neutrons </obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>Kr-84 ion;Kr-84 nucleus;Kr-84 </obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>Krypton-84 nucleus</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005066 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005066">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UO_0000129"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000111>mGy/sec</obo:IAO_0000111>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A derived absorbed dose unit which is equal to one milligray absorbed per second</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>mGy/sec</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>UO:0010065</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:label>miligray per second</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005067 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005067">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0051716"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000111>Indirect cellular response to stimulus</obo:IAO_0000111>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>This could be used to describe a tissue response to immune stimulation, so for example an IL2 response of endothelial cells in response to lymphocytes stimulated by pathogen.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that results in a change in state or activity of a cell (in terms of movement, secretion, enzyme production, gene expression, etc.) as a result of a stimulus which acts on another cell and not on the responding cell. The process begins with detection of the stimulus by a cell and ends with a change in state or activity of a different cell</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>Paul Schofield</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>indirect cellular response to stimulus</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005068 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005068">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005067"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>The most obvious example is the bystander effect ( Carmel Mothersill, Andrej Rusin, Cristian Fernandez-Palomo &amp; Colin Seymour (2018) History of bystander effects research 1905-present; what is in a name?, International Journal of Radiation Biology, 94:8, 696-707, DOI: 10.1080/09553002.2017.1398436) where a naive cell behaves as if it has been irradiated because of its communication with an irradiated cell. It may also be used to describe the abscopal effect. Note that abscopal effects have been noted for ionising and non-ionising ( RF) radiation, eg. Löffler  MW. et al. . A Non-interventional Clinical Trial Assessing Immune Responses After Radiofrequency Ablation of Liver Metastases From Colorectal Cancer. Front Immunol. 2019 Nov 19;10:2526. doi: 10.3389/fimmu.2019.02526. PMID: 31803175; PMCID: PMC6877671</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that results in a change in state or activity of a cell (in terms of movement, secretion, enzyme production, gene expression, etc.) as a result of radiation acting on another cell and not on the responding cell. The process begins with detection of radiation by a cell or cells and ends with a change in state or activity of a different cell or cells.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>Paul Schofield</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>indirect cellular response to radiation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005069 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005069">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005068"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Directly describes the bystander effect (Carmel Mothersill, Andrej Rusin, Cristian Fernandez-Palomo &amp; Colin Seymour (2018) History of bystander effects research 1905-present; what is in a name?, International Journal of Radiation Biology, 94:8, 696-707, DOI: 10.1080/09553002.2017.1398436) but can alo sbe used to describe phenomena related to the abscopal effect, manifest in whole organisms where irradiation of one part of the organism causes changes in another un-irradiated component.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that results in a change in state or activity of a cell (in terms of movement, secretion, enzyme production, gene expression, etc.) as a result of exposure to ionising radiation which acts on another cell and not on the responding cell. The process begins with detection of the ionising radiation by one cell or cells and ends with a change in state or activity of a different cell or cells.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>Paul Schofield</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>indirect cellular response to ionising radiation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005070 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005070">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005014"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A device for exposing materials or biological organisms to ionizing radiation for research or therapeutic purposes.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>ionizing radiation device</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005071 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005071">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ENVO_01001488"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>High-altitude balloons are crewed or uncrewed balloons, usually filled with helium or hydrogen, that are released into the stratosphere, generally attaining between 18 and 37 km (11 and 23 mi; 59,000 and 121,000 ft) above sea level.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>Wikipedia</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>high-altitude balloon</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005072 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005072">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002062"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000111>spaceflight</obo:IAO_0000111>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Travel beyond the Earth&apos;s atmosphere</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>space flight</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005074 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005074">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005069"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000111>non-targeted effects of ionising radiation</obo:IAO_0000111>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Non-targeted effects of ionising radiation are characterised by cellular responses that occur in cells in which energy from ionizing radiation has not been deposited. The distinct classes of non-targeted effects include Genomic Instability, and Bystander effect which latter includes Abscopal effects and Bystander-mediated adaptive response. These can also arise non-clonally in the progeny of irradiated somatic or germ cells, resulting in radiation-induced genomic instability and transgenerational genomic instability respectively. (Kadhim M, Salomaa S, Wright E, et al. Non-targeted effects of ionising radiation--implications for low dose risk. Mutat Res. 2013;752(2):84-98. doi:10.1016/j.mrrev.2012.12.001)</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Paul Schofield ORCID: 0000-0002-5111-7263</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>NTE</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>non-targeted effects</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>non-targeted effects of ionising radiation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010000 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010000">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000104"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000111>ALARA</obo:IAO_0000111>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>One of the measures to ensure ALARA is to minimize the time spent working around radioactive materials.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A principle of radiation protection philosophy that requires that exposures to ionizing radiation be kept as low as reasonably achievable, economic and social factors being taken into account. The protection from radiation exposure is ALARA when the expen</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>ALARA</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>ALARA</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>as low as reasonably achievable</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010001 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010001">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000182"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Attenuation in concrete or leadshielding is a common method of radiation protection on Earth, but is impractical in space due to mass constraints.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The reduction of radiation intensity upon passage of radiation through matter</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>radiation attenuation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010002 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010002">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHMO_0002796"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Biodosimetry was used to measure the exposure of some victims of the Chernobyl nuclear accident.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Use of a biological response as an indicator of a dose of an effective agent (e.g., the extent of decline in peripheral blood lymphocytes of humans exposed to ionizing radiation can be used as an indicator of the absorbed dose to the whole body from that</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>biodosimetry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010003 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010003">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005074"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>The bystander effect can be studied by identifying cells that were traversed by a charged particle.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The term used to describe the effects on cells in proximity to irradiated cells, but through which radiation has not passed. In experimental radiation biology, the bystander effect can be investigated by targeting individual cells, or by using doses low enough that the probability for a given cell to be hit by a particle is less than one.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>bystander effect</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010006 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010006">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000117"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Delta ray energies increase with the velocity of the incident particle.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Highly energetic electrons produced during inelastic collisions between ionizing radiation and atomic electrons. In a small proportion of collisions, the ejected electron receives a considerable amount of energy (i.e., &gt;1,000 eV), allowing it to travel a</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>delta electron</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>delta electron</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>delta ray</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010007 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010007">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010016"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>The average annual dose equivalent for crew members on the ISS is approximately 300 mSv.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The product of the absorbed dose (D) at a point and the quality factor (Q) at that point for the radiation type (i.e., H = DQ). The unit of H is J-kg^[-1] with the special name sievert (Sv)</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>dose equivalent</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010008 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010008">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000119"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The increased risk from induced cancer due to exposure when the effects over an entire lifetime are accounted for. Individuals who would have died of cancer anyway but die early because of exposure are not included</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>excess lifetime risk of cancer</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010009 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010009">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015001"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>The sample was irradiated with fast neutrons.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Neutrons of energies above a few hundred kiloelectron volts (keV)</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>fast neutron</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010010 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010010">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000109"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>The value of the fluence was approximately 1 particle per cm2.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The number of particles or photons per unit of cross-sectional area perpendicular to the direction of the particle (units of m^2 or cm^2) (i.e., particles-m^[-2] or particles-cm^[-2]).</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>fluence</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010011 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010011">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000109"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>The flux of cosmic ray muons per cm2 at the surface of the Earth is approximately 1.0.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The quotient of dN by dt, where dN is the increment of the particle number in the time interval dt. The unit for flux is reciprocal second (s^[-1].)</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>flux</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010012 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010012">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_33252"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005003"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An atomic nucleus, fully stripped of its orbital electrons, containing 2 or more protons. Nuclei containing between 2 protons (helium) and 6 protons (carbon) are sometimes referred to as light ions.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>In RBO--needs def from NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>heavy ion</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010013 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010013">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000109"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Biological effects are a function of LET.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The energy deposited by an ionizing charged particle or photon in any medium per unit path length.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>LET</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>LET</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>linear energy transfer</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010014 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010014">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010016"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Radiation protection standards take into account organ dose as well as whole body dose.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The mean absorbed radiation dose in a specified tissue or organ of the human body.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>organ dose</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010015 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010015">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001018"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Neutrons have a high quality factor compared to photons.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Dimensionless factor developed for purposes of radiation protection and assessing health risks in general terms that accounts for the relative biological effectiveness of different radiations in producing stochastic effects and is used to relate absorbed</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>quality factor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010016 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010016">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OBI_0000984"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>The samples received a dose of 1 Gy. (The use of the gray unit implies that &quot;dose&quot; refers to a measured quantity, typically absorbed dose or organ dose.)</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>The samples received a dose of 1 Sv. (The use of the sievert unit implies that &quot;dose&quot; refers to a der</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A measurement datum of the quantity of radiation absorbed by a substance or organism. The term &quot;radiation dose&quot; may for example refer to absorbed dose, organ dose, dose equivalent or equivalent dose, depending upon context and weighting factor(s).</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>radiation dose</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010018 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010018">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OBI_0001909"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Radiation weighting factors may change as more data on biological effects are obtained.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A factor used to allow for differences in the biological effectiveness between different radiations when calculating equivalent dose (H_T) (see equivalent dose). These factors are independent of the tissue or organ irradiated.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>radiation weighting factor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010019 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010019">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000109"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Neutrons have been found to have a higher RBE than electrons.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Factor used to compare the biological effectiveness of absorbed doses from different types of ionizing radiation, determined experimentally. RBE is the ratio of the absorbed dose of a reference radiation to the absorbed dose of the radiation in question r</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>RBE</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>RBE</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>relative biological effectiveness</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010020 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010020">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001018"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Tissue equivalent materials can be used to study radiation effects at depth in the human body.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Denotes a substance with absorbing and scattering properties for a given radiation that sufficiently match those of a particular biological tissue.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>tissue equivalence</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010022 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010022">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000011"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An active dosimeter for radon.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>active radon dosimeter</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010023 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010023">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000012"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An passive dosimeter for radon.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>passive radon dosimeter</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010024 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010024">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010022"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An active radon dosimeter that uses a phosphor-coated zinc sulfide screen or a liquid scintillator that emits light when hit by alpha particles from radon decay. The emitted light is measured with a photomultiplier tube. They have high sensitivity, fast response time.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>scintillation radon dosimeter</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010025 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010025">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010026"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An active radon dosimeter that measure the concentration of radon decay products (polonium-218, lead-214, and bismuth-214) using alpha spectroscopy or particle collection on a filter. They provide working level (WL) values, which indicate the potential radiation dose from radon decay products.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>Provides direct measurement of biologically relevant radiation exposure. Expensive, mainly used in occupational settings.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>CWLM</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>continuous working level radon dosimeter</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010026 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010026">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010022"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An active radon dosimeter that uses various sensors (e.g., scintillation detectors, ion chambers, or solid-state silicon detectors) to measure radon decay in real-time. They typically record radon levels at hourly intervals.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>Provides near real-time results, detects fluctuations over time, useful for home inspections.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>CRM</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>continuous radon monitor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010027 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010027">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010023"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A passive radon dosimeter that contain an electret (a charged Teflon disc) inside an ionization chamber. Radon decay ionizes the air, causing a measurable voltage drop in the electret, which correlates with radon concentration.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>Can be read without a lab, provides both short- and long-term measurements.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <rdfs:label>electret ion chamber radon dosimeter</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010028 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010028">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010023"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A passive radon dosimeter in which a container filled with activated charcoal absorbs radon from the air. After exposure (typically 2–7 days), the canister is sealed and sent to a lab for gamma spectroscopy, which measures radon decay products.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>charcoal cannister radon dosimeter</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010029 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010029">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010023"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A passive radon dosimeter in which a small plastic film or polycarbonate chip is exposed to radon gas. When radon decays, it emits alpha particles that leave microscopic tracks on the film. After exposure, the film is chemically etched to reveal the tracks, which are counted under a microscope to determine radon levels.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>Alpha track radon dosimeters are used domestically for radon monitoring over long periods of time.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>alpha track detector</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>alpha track radon dosimeter</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010030 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010030">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000122"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Nuclear reactors primarily used to produce neutrons for the study of materials at the atomic level, the biological effects of radiation, the production of radioisotopes for medicine, industry and research, and the imaging of objects’ internal structure.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>research reactor</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>Definition adapted from: https://www.iaea.org/bulletin/what-are-research-reactors-how-do-they-contribute-to-sustainable-development</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>research nuclear reactor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010031 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010031">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000122"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A nuclear reactor primarily used to generate and supply power.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>This conceptualization of nuclear power reactor emphasizes its role as a radiation source.  For use to emphasize its roles in energy production, recommend use of identically named concept ENVO:02000137</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>power reactor</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>nuclear power reactor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010032 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010032">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000122"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A nuclear reactor used for military purposes such as plutonium production.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>military nuclear reactor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010033 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010033">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000122"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A nuclear reactor used for more than one purpose.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>multi-purpose nuclear reactor</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010034 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010034">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010037"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An 160 kV x-ray source.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>doi: 10.2349/biij.2.1.e1</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>faxitron CP160</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010035 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010035">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010038"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An 320 kV x-ray source.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://precisionxray.com/x-rad/xrad-320/</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>x-rad320</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010036 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010036">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005039"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A source of gamma irradiation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>http://www.theratronics.ca/PDFs/GC40_BTMB_8008GC40E_2_v112013_webSECURE.pdf</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>gammacell 40</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010037 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010037">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OBI_0001138"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An x-ray source operating at a voltage of 160 kV.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>160 kV x-ray device</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010038 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010038">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OBI_0001138"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An x-ray source operating at a voltage of 320 kV.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>320 kV x-ray device</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010039 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010039">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ENVO_01001406"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A facility designed and constructed so as to minimize exposure to natural background radiation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>low background facility</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010040 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010040">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010039"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A facility located below the earth&apos;s surface in order to minimize natural background radiation</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>underground low background radiation laboratory</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010041 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010041">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ENVO_01001852"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An energy transfer process in which radiation intensity is reduced by modifying the composition, and/or energy of incident radiation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>radiation shielding</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010042 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010042">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010045"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Radiation shielding that require using an external power or energy source, such as an electrostatic or electromagnetic field.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>active radiation shielding</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010043 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010043">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010045"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The role of materials used in radiation shielding such as concrete, lead, or polyethylene, with no requirement for external power or operator intervention</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>passive radiation shielding role</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010044 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010044">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001018"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The power per unit solid angle delivered by radiation incident on a given location.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>radiation intensity</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010045 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010045">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000023"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The role of a material in reducing radiation intensity by modifying the composition, and/or energy of incident radiation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>radiation shielding role</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010046 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010046">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010041"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Radiation shielding using materials such as concrete, lead, or polyethylene, with no requirement for external power or operator intervention</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>passive radiation shielding</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010047 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010047">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/DOID_4"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A serious human disease that occurs when the entire body (or a large portion of it) is exposed to a high dose of penetrating radiation—typically more than 1 Gray (Gy)—over a short period (minutes to hours).Key features: Prodromal Phase (hours to days): Nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, fatigue. Latent Phase (hours to weeks): Apparent improvement or absence of symptoms. Manifest Illness Phase: Depending on dose, damage to rapidly dividing cells leads to one or more syndromes: Hematopoietic syndrome (1–10 Gy): Bone marrow failure, infection, bleeding. Gastrointestinal syndrome (5–20 Gy): Severe GI tract damage, fluid loss, sepsis. Neurovascular syndrome (≥20 Gy): Neurological impairment, confusion, convulsions; usually fatal within days. Recovery or Death: If dose is survivable and supportive care is provided, recovery can occur over weeks to months; higher doses carry a poor prognosis.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>acute radiation sickness, ARS</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>Dainiak, N., &amp; Albanese, J. (2022). Medical management of acute radiation syndrome. Journal of radiological protection : official journal of the Society for Radiological Protection, 42(3), 10.1088/1361-6498/ac7d18. https://doi.org/10.1088/1361-6498/ac7d18. https://www.icrpaedia.org/Acute_radiation_syndrome</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>acute radiation syndrome</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010048 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010048">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000016"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An environment in which there is human-created radiation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>Includes terrestrial and non-terrestrial environments. Includes environments created for investigations, and those used for other purposes including those created by humans for habitation, manufacture, storage, clinical practice and transport. Will include mines and other environments actively created by humans by the manipulation of natural materials</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <rdfs:label>anthropogenic radiation environment</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010049 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010049">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000016"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A radiation environment resulting from natural processes.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>natural radiation environment</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010050 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010050">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000016"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The radiation environment below the surface of the Earth, consisting of radiation from naturally occurring radioactive elements; particles originating in space which are sufficiently energetic and/or weakly interacting with the Earth&apos;s magnetic field, atmosphere and materials in the Earth&apos;s crust to enable them to penetrate below the Earth&apos;s surface; particles produced by interactions of primary radiation with the Earth&apos;s crust; and anthropogenic sources of radiation such as buried nuclear waste. The composition and strength of the radiation at a given point under the Earth&apos;s surface depends strongly on depth below the surface and composition of the surrounding crustal material.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>terrestrial subsurface radiation environment</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>earth subsurface radiation environment</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010051 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010051">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000016"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115> The radiation environment at the surface of the Earth, consisting of particles originating in space which are sufficiently energetic and/or weakly interacting with the Earth&apos;s magnetic field and atmosphere to reach the surface; particles produced by interactions of space radiation with the Earth&apos;s atmosphere; decays of naturally occurring radioactive elements on the surface; and anthropogenic radiation. The composition and strength of the radiation at a given point on the Earth&apos;s surface depends on factors including altitude, the composition of the surface material, and the temporal variation in incident space radiation, e.g. with solar cycle.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>terrestrial surface radiation environment</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>earth surface radiation environment</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010052 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010052">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000016"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The radiation environment in the Earth&apos;s atmosphere , consisting of particles originating in space which are sufficiently energetic and/or weakly interacting to penetrate the Earth&apos;s magnetic field; particles produced by interactions of space radiation with the Earth&apos;s atmosphere and albedo radiation from interactions of incident radiation with the Earth&apos;s crust; and anthropogenic radiation. The composition and strength of the radiation at a given point in the Earth&apos;s atmosphere depends on factors including altitude and the temporal variation in incident space radiation, e.g. with solar cycle.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>terrestrial atmosphere radiation environment</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>earth atmosphere radiation environment</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010053 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010053">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001739"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The quality of neutrons or charged particles, such as protons or alpha particles, such that they can produce ionizing events densely spaced on a molecular scale (e.g., &gt;10 keV-micron^[-1].</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>high linear energy transfer</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010054 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010054">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001739"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The quality of X and gamma rays or light charged particles such as electrons such that they can only produce sparse ionizing events far apart on a molecular scale.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>low linear energy transfer</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010055 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010055">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000082"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Microdosimetry is a radiobiology study type that deals with the study of the distribution of energy deposition on a micrometer scale in biological systems exposed to ionizing radiation. It focuses on understanding how radiation interacts with cellular and sub-cellular structures and the resulting effects on biological systems.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>&quot;For example, chromosomal domains up to nuclei, and of the associated probability distribution&quot;</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>microdosimetry</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>microdosimetry study type</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010056 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010056">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000082"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A study of the biological effects of low acute or protracted doses of ionising radiation defined as being below 500 mSv, less than 100mGy, or at dose rates less than 5mGy per hour. ( https://www.nationalacademies.org/our-work/developing-a-long-term-strategy-for-low-dose-radiation-research-in-the-united-states)</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>low dose radiation study type</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010057 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010057">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010051"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The radiation environment consisting of the water at the surface of the Earth, including all of Earth’s water: oceans and seas, lakes and rivers (freshwater), groundwater, and atmospheric water. Some frameworks treat the cryosphere (ice, snow, glaciers) as a sub-part of the hydrosphere. Radiation in water consists of particles originating in space which are sufficiently energetic and/or weakly interacting with the Earth&apos;s magnetic field and atmosphere to reach the surface; particles produced by interactions of space radiation with the Earth&apos;s atmosphere; decays of naturally occurring radioactive elements dissolved or suspended in water, those on the substrate where there is body of water and from the subsurface. This includes anthropogenic radiation carried in water by means of direct discharges, precipitation and other transfer routes. The composition and strength of the radiation in water at a given point on the Earth&apos;s surface depends on factors including underlying geology, water chemistry and elemental composition, and the temporal variation in incident space radiation, e.g. with solar cycle.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>earth hydrosphere radiation environment</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010058 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010058">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000109"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A measurement of the amount of energy deposited by ionizing radiation per unit length of a medium. . It represents the average energy transferred to the material per unit length along the path of the particle in a specified micrometric ( or smaller) site divided by the mean chord length of the site volume. Lineal energy is typically expressed in units of keV/?m (kilo-electron volts per micrometer) or MeV/cm (mega-electron volts per centimeter)</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>lineal energy measurement datum</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010059 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010059">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001241"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The ionization cluster size is a measure of the spatial extent or size of the ionized region created by the passage of radiation through a medium. It measures the number of ion pairs that are produced within a localized volume or track of the radiation particle.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>In the case of ionization cluster size per unit length, the common unit is &quot;ion pairs per micrometer&quot; (ion pairs/µm) or &quot;ion pairs per millimeter&quot; (ion pairs/mm). This unit represents the average number of ion pairs produced within a length of one micrometer or one millimeter along the path of the ionizing particle. For ionization cluster size per unit volume, the unit may be &quot;ion pairs per cubic micrometer&quot; (ion pairs/µm³) or &quot;ion pairs per cubic millimeter&quot; (ion pairs/mm³). This unit represents the average number of ion pairs produced within a volume of one cubic micrometer or one cubic millimeter.&quot;</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>ionisation cluster size</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>ionization cluster size</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010060 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010060">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000032"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A measure of the spatial extent or size of the ionized region created by the passage of radiation through a medium. It measures the number of ion pairs that are produced within a localized volume or track of the radiation particle.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>In the case of ionization cluster size per unit length, the common unit is &quot;ion pairs per micrometer&quot; (ion pairs/µm) or &quot;ion pairs per millimeter&quot; (ion pairs/mm). This unit represents the average number of ion pairs produced within a length of one micrometer or one millimeter along the path of the ionizing particle. For ionization cluster size per unit volume, the unit may be &quot;ion pairs per cubic micrometer&quot; (ion pairs/µm³) or &quot;ion pairs per cubic millimeter&quot; (ion pairs/mm³). This unit represents the average number of ion pairs produced within a volume of one cubic micrometer or one cubic millimeter.&quot;</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>ionisation cluster size measurement datum</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>ionization cluster size measurement datum</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010061 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010061">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000006"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The particle track penumbra is the region where secondary electrons released by the primary particle, transitioning a medium, are the exclusive agents of energy deposition due to multiple scattering and energy loss of the charged particles as they interact with the atoms and molecules of the medium.&quot; Makes sense to define this a s a spatial region ( BFO:000006) as it has extent that can be measured but is a concept rather than a material entity.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>Heavy nuclei of the primary galactic radiation in space can have the same linear energy transfer yet greatly different lateral distribution patterns of the energy in the microstructure of tissue. Track structure thus presents itself as a new dosimetric parameter for HZE particles which is at present incompletely understood in its radiobiological significance. The theory of track structure distinguishes two regions: core and penumbra. The core is a narrow region with a radius far below 1 micron in tissue where energy deposition occurs mainly through excitations and collective oscillations of electrons. Energy density in the core accounts for slightly more than half the total LET. The penumbra surrounding the core extends laterally several to many microns depending on the energy of the primary. Energy density in the penumbra decreases steeply with the square of increasing radius. The relationships are illustrated with nuclear emulsion micrographs and plots of energy density profiles. The implications of the findings for a dosimetric system for HZE particles are discussed. See Chatterjee, A., Schaefer, H.J. ( 1976) Rad Env Biophys 13, 215-227  https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19750020623/downloads/19750020623.pdf</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <rdfs:label>particle track penumbra</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010062 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010062">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000006"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>In the theory of particle track structure, the narrow central zone with a radius in matter far below 1 micron where energy deposition occurs mainly in processes of ionisation, excitation and collective oscillation of the atoms of the medium related to the passage of the primary particle.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>particle track core</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010063 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010063">
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A volume characterized by dimensions on the order of 1-100 nanometers</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>nanometric volume</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010064 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010064">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000000"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A processual entity that is one discrete interaction of an incident particle and atomic or sub-atomic constituents of matter, in which a quantized amount of energy is transferred to atomic or sub-atomic constituents of matter, producing excitation, ionization, or nuclear recoil</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>energy deposition event</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010065 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010065">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0000125"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The total mass of an atom, equal to the mass of the atom&apos;s constituent protons, neutrons and electrons minus the nuclear and electron binding energies. Atomic mass is expressed in atomic mass units (amu), where 1 amu is equal to one twelfth the mass of an atom of carbon-12, which is equal to 1.660539040 x 10^(-24) gram. The amu is also called the Dalton (Da).</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>atomic mass</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010067 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010067">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0000125"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The weighted average of the atomic masses of the naturally occurring stable isotopes and long-lived radioactive isotopes of an element. Atomic weight is typically expressed in atomic mass units. For example, the atomic weight of carbon, 12.011 amu, is calculated based on the natural abundance of carbon-12 (approx. 98.9%) and carbon-13 (approx. 1.1%), with carbon-14 contributing a negligible amount.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>atomic weight</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011000 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011000">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000040"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>Primary parent for physical infrastructure assets.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <rdfs:comment>Paul Schofield</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Physical radiation research infrastructure</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>A material entity such as physical resources, facilities, or personnel organized to support radiation biology research.</skos:definition>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011001 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011001">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000030"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>Primary parent for digital/information infrastructure assets.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <rdfs:comment>Paul Schofield</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Digital radiation research infrastructure</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>An information content entity that consists of digital resources, data systems, and models organized to support radiation biology research.</skos:definition>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011002 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011002">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ENVO_03501288"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011000"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Paul Schofield</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>radiation exposure facility</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>A facility specifically designed to provide controlled radiation exposure to biological or physical samples for research purposes.</skos:definition>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011003 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011003">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011002"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Paul Schofield</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>external exposure facility</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>A radiation exposure facility in which biological or physical samples are irradiated by a radiation source external to the subject, including photon, particle beam, neutron, radon and calibration irradiation systems.</skos:definition>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011004 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011004">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011002"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Paul Schofield</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>internal exposure facility</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>A radiation exposure facility in which biological subjects are exposed to radionuclides via incorporation routes such as inhalation, ingestion or wound contamination, resulting in internal radiation exposure from incorporated radionuclides.</skos:definition>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011005 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011005">
        <obo:IAO_0000116>Polyhierarchy: child of both Physical Infrastructure and BFO:Site.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <rdfs:comment>Paul Schofield</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>contaminated site observatory</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>A geographically defined area contaminated with radionuclides or naturally occurring radioactive materials (NORM) that is formally designated as a long-term field research site for radioecological investigation.</skos:definition>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011006 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011006">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000027"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011000"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Paul Schofield</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>radiation research analytical platform</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>A facility providing specialised analytical capabilities including biodosimetry, genomics, proteomics, radiochemistry, in vivo bioassay or imaging used to characterise biological samples or measure radiation-related outcomes in support of radiation biology and radiation protection research.</skos:definition>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011007 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011007">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OBIB_0000616"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011000"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Paul Schofield</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>radiation research sample bank</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>A physical infrastructure for the systematic storage and management of biological or environmental samples related to radiation research.</skos:definition>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011008 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011008">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011001"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Paul Schofield</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>radiation epidemiology cohort</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>A research infrastructure centered around a defined group of individuals (aggregate of organisms) tracked over time for radiation-related health outcomes.</skos:definition>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011009 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011009">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011001"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Paul Schofield</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>radiation biology database</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>An information content entity consisting of structured collections of data related to radiation biology research.</skos:definition>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011010 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011010">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011001"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Paul Schofield</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>radiation dosimetry model</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>A computational model, simulation code or software application used to calculate radiation doses, model the biological effects of ionising radiation, assess environmental radiation risk, or support radiological emergency response decision-making.</skos:definition>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011011 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011011">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/COB_0001300"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00011000"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Paul Schofield</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>radiation dosimetry tool</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>A physical infrastructure consisting of devices or instruments used to measure radiation dose.</skos:definition>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015000 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015000">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000040"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>That energy which is transferred in space and time as part of the process of radiation (ENVO:01001023).</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>radiation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015001 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015001">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015000"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Uncharged  particles with kinetic energy imparted by natural or artificial means (such as by a particle accelerator)</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>neutral particle radiation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015002 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015002">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005041"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An assay of the pattern (frequency and spatial distribution) of ionization events produced by energy deposition in time and space in a substance characterized by linear dimensions of the order of 1-100 nanometers</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>&quot;For example, assay of DNA, and of the associated probability distribution&quot;</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>nanodosimetry</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>See H.H. Rossi, Microdosimetry and Radiobiology, Radiation Protection Dosimetry, Volume 13, Issue 1-4, 1 December 1985, Pages 259ï¿½265, https://doi.org/10.1093/rpd/13.1-4.259 and Gustavo A. Santa Cruz,Microdosimetry: Principles and applications,Reports of Practical Oncology &amp; Radiotherapy,Volume 21, Issue 2,2016,Pages 135-139,ISSN 1507-1367,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rpor.2014.10.006.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment>Usually a track structure assay at nm scale</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>nanodosimetry assay</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015003 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015003">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005041"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An assay of the pattern (frequency and spatial distribution) of ionization events produced by energy deposition in time and space in a substance characterized by linear dimensions of the order of 1-100 micrometers</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>&quot;For example, chromosomal domains up to nuclei, and of the associated probability distribution&quot;</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>microdosimetry</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>See H.H. Rossi, Microdosimetry and Radiobiology, Radiation Protection Dosimetry, Volume 13, Issue 1-4, 1 December 1985, Pages 259ï¿½265, https://doi.org/10.1093/rpd/13.1-4.259 and Gustavo A. Santa Cruz,Microdosimetry: Principles and applications,Reports of Practical Oncology &amp; Radiotherapy,Volume 21, Issue 2,2016,Pages 135-139,ISSN 1507-1367,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rpor.2014.10.006.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment>Usually a track structure assay at micrometre scale</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>microdosimetry assay</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015004 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015004">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00002118"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Nuclear decay in which an alpha particle is emitted.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00005</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/REX_0000017</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>alpha-decay</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>alpha decay</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015005 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015005">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00002118"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Radioactive decay in which a beta particle is emitted or in which orbital electron capture occurs.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>http://goldbook.iupac.org/B00572.html</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/REX_0000007</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>beta-decay</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>beta decay</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015006 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015006">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000015"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Processes in which electrons or components of the atomic nucleus are participants.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>subatomic process</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015007 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015007">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010016"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Measurement of the pattern (frequency and spatial distribution) of ionization events produced by energy deposition in time and space in a substance characterized by linear dimensions of the order of 1-100 nanometers</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>nanodosimetric measurement datum</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015008 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015008">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010016"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>In microdosimetry the specific energy, z, is the energy deposited by radiation ( in one or more deposition events) in a specified micrometric (or smaller) site divided my the mass of the site. Measured in Gy.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>specific energy measurement datum</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015009 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015009">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000030"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The path of a particle in matter, delineated by sites where the particle deposits energy.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>particle track</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015010 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015010">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001241"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A pattern (frequency and spatial distribution) of ionization events produced by energy deposition in time and space in a substance characterized by linear dimensions of the order of 1-100 nanometers.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>nanodosimetry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015011 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015011">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001236"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The complete set of spatial coordinates of energy deposition events along a particle track in matter</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>particle track structure</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015012 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015012">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000000"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A group of ionization events closely related in time and space, occurring in a specified target volume and originating from a single primary ionizing particle.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>ionization cluster</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015013 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015013">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010016"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Measurement of the pattern (frequency and spatial distribution) of ionization events produced by energy deposition in time and space in a substance characterized by linear dimensions of the order of 1-100 micrometers.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>microdosimetric measurement datum</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015014 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015014">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001241"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A volume of matter characterised by linear dimensions of the order of 0.5- 10&apos;s of microns, referred to as the &quot;site&quot; in microdosimetry.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>micrometric volume</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015015 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015015">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OBI_0000112"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A control specimen role of a biotic or abiotic sample or individual describes a specimen by its purpose, and therefore treatment, within a planned experimental process that tests hypotheses by isolating variables as dictated by the scientific method in order to make a conclusion about the effect of such variables. The quality of control inhering in the sample role exists as a result of the historical treatment of the sample. In a controlled experiment, two or more virtually identical experiments are conducted, but the factor being tested is varied in only one of them. This serves to isolate any causal phenomena. A control may have been subject to treatments during the experiment that might alter its state or behaviour and is therefore distinguishable from the sample as initial input into the experiment. However such treatments should in all cases but that of the variable under scrutiny, be the same as the experimental sample.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>http://ncicb.nci.nih.gov/xml/owl/EVS/Thesaurus.owl#C64355</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:label>control specimen role</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015016 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015016">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005015"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Sham irradiation in which an identical irradiation protocol (with the exception of the administration of the radiation exposure) is not followed in every respect.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:comment>Use approximate sham irradiation when at least one aspect of the irradiation (with the exception of the administration of the radiation exposure) is not followed precisely. For example, Not placing an organism or sample in a beam line for the same length of time as the irradiated organism(s) or sample(s) were left in the beam.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>approximate sham irradiation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015017 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015017">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00002000"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Experimental internal radiation exposure of rodents to radon gas through inhalation.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Exposure to an inhaled, ingested, injected or implanted source of radiation of any origin as part of a planned or accidental process.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>internal radiation exposure</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015018 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015018">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00002000"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112> Mice exposed to external radiation from a Sr source.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Exposure to an external source of radiation of any origin or type.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>external radiation exposure</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015019 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015019">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005015"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Sham irradiation in which an identical irradiation protocol is followed in every respect, with the exception of the administration of the radiation exposure.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:comment>Use complete sham irradiation when all steps of an irradiation protocol are followed for sham control group (with the exception of administration of the radiation exposure).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>complete sham irradiation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015020 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015020">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00002008"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015017"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Planned exposure of an entity to radiation of any type, for example as part of a medical or experimental procedure with the intention of exposing the entity to radiation energy internally by the ingestion, inhalation or implantation of a source of radiation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:comment> Research subjects participating in a clinical trial using an internally implanted radiation source have an internal experimental radiation exposure</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>internal experimental radiation exposure</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015021 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015021">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015006"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The direct or indirect transfer of energy from radiation to a medium through ionisation or excitation of the atoms of the medium.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>energy deposition</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015022 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015022">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000015"/>
        <rdfs:label>track formation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015023 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015023">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000082"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A mixed irradiation study typically involves exposing a sample or system to a combination of different types of radiation. This could include exposure to various forms of ionizing radiation, such as gamma rays, X-rays, and particle radiation. Exposure might be simultaneous or sequential and from anthropogenic or natural sources of radiation. The goal is to understand how the different types of radiation interact with each other and their combined effects on the material or biological system under investigation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>mixed irradiation study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015024 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015024">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015023"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A mixed ion and photon irradiation study typically involves exposing a sample or system to a combination of these types of radiation. This could include exposure to various forms of ionizing radiation, such as gamma rays, X-rays, and particle radiation. Exposure might be simultaneous or sequential and from anthropogenic or natural sources of radiation. The goal is to understand how the different types of radiation interact with each other and their combined effects on the material or biological system under investigation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>mixed ion and photon irradiation study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015025 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015025">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015024"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A mixed ion and photon simultaneous irradiation study typically involves simultaneously exposing a sample or system to a combination of these types of radiation. This could include exposure to various forms of ionizing radiation, such as gamma rays, X-rays, and particle radiation. Exposure might be from anthropogenic or natural sources of radiation. The goal is to understand how the different types of radiation interact with each other and their combined effects on the material or biological system under investigation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>mixed ion and photon simultaneous irradiation study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015026 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015026">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015024"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A mixed ion and photon sequential irradiation study typically involves sequentially exposing a sample or system to a combination of these types of radiation. This could include exposure to various forms of ionizing radiation, such as gamma rays, X-rays, and particle radiation. Exposure might be from anthropogenic or natural sources of radiation. The goal is to understand how the different types of radiation interact with each other and their combined effects on the material or biological system under investigation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>mixed ion and photon sequential irradiation study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015027 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015027">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015023"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A multiple ion irradiation study typically involves exposing a sample or system to a combination of different types of charged particle radiation. Exposure might be simultaneous or sequential and from anthropogenic or natural sources of radiation. The goal is to understand how the different types of radiation interact with each other and their combined effects on the material or biological system under investigation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>multiple ion mixed irradiation study</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>multiple ion irradiation study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015028 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015028">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015027"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A multiple ion simultaneous irradiation study typically involves simultaneously exposing a sample or system to a combination of ions. This could include exposure to various forms of particle radiation, for example ion beams. Exposure might be from anthropogenic or natural sources of radiation. The goal is to understand how the different types of radiation interact with each other and their combined effects on the material or biological system under investigation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>multiple ion simultaneous irradiation study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015029 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015029">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015027"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A multiple ion sequential irradiation study involves exposing a sample or system to a combination of different ions. This could include exposure to various forms of particle radiation such as ion beams. Exposure might be from anthropogenic or natural sources of radiation. The goal is to understand how the different types of radiation interact with each other and their combined effects on the material or biological system under investigation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>multiple ion sequential irradiation study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015030 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015030">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000013"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Radiation present in an experimental or therapeutic process, other than that produced as part of that planned process.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>ambient radiation</oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>background radiation</oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>anthropogenic ionizing background radiation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015031 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015031">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000013"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Radiation originating in the terrestrial or extra-terrestrial environment.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>natural ionizing background radiation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015032 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015032">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0000025"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001739"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000111>beam composition</obo:IAO_0000111>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The types and characteristics of particles or waves that make up the radiation produced or emitted by a radiation source for use in a particular exposure context or protocol. The source may be artificial as in a particle accelerator or natural, in a controlled environment, such as a collimated beam from a radioactive isotope.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>radiation beam composition</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015033 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015033">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000027"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>The space radiation field in the solar system consists of particle and electromagnetic radiation from the sun, electromagnetic radiation originating from extra-solar sources, and galactic cosmic radiation.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The mix of charged and neutral atomic and subatomic particles and electromagnetic radiation suffusing a defined volume in an extraterrestrial environment at a given time.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>space radiation field</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015034 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015034">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00002000"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The process by which an entity is exposed to two or more species of accelerated ions sequentially or simultaneously.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Mark H. Phillips</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0003-4312-9552</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1741-8297</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>mixed irradiation with multiple ions</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>multiple ion mixed irradiation</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:comment>The term is often used as jargon, in which cases it is taken to imply sequential exposure to multiple ion types, and not parallel exposures. (perhaps include reference to NSRL)</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>multiple ion irradiation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015035 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015035">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0000025"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Radiation field composition refers to the spectrum of types and characteristics of radiation present in a specific area or region where radiation is being used, emitted, absorbed, or measured. It describes the combination of different types of particles or waves that make up the radiation in a given field.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0003-4312-9552</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1741-8297</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>radiation field composition</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015036 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015036">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015001"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000111>thermal neutron</obo:IAO_0000111>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A neutron that has (by collision with other particles) reached an energy state equal to that of its surroundings, typically on the order of 0.025 eV.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263, https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://www.nrc.gov/reading-rm/basic-ref/glossary/neutron-thermal.html</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>thermal neutron</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015037 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015037">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015001"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000111>epithermal neutron</obo:IAO_0000111>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>a neutron with energy greater than a thermal neutron, but less than a fast neutron. The approximate energy range is 0.025-0.4 eV.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263,https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://ns.ph.liv.ac.uk/~ajb/radiometrics/glossary/epithermal_neutron.html</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>epithermal neutron</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015038 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015038">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015000"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000111>background radiation</obo:IAO_0000111>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The ambient radiation in a defined environment emanating from natural or anthropogenic sources.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>For uses involving experimentation or radiation therapy, use &quot;anthropogenic background radiation&quot; instead. Use this concept to indicate source is a mixture of anthropogenic and naturally occurring (ambient), or unknown or if the discrimination is not sought.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263, https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>background radiation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015039 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015039">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015038"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000111>non-ionizing background radiation</obo:IAO_0000111>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Background radiation that does not result in ionization of target material</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263, https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>non-ionizing background radiation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015040 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015040">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015039"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000111>anthropogenic non-ionizing background radiation</obo:IAO_0000111>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Non-ionizing background radiation that is the result of a human planned process.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263, https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>anthropogenic non-ionizing background radiation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015041 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015041">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015039"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000111>natural non-ionizing background radiation</obo:IAO_0000111>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Non-ionizing background radiation that is not the result of a human planned process.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263, https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>natural non-ionizing background radiation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015042 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015042">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000016"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The toroidal zones surrounding the Earth, comprised of charged particles trapped by the Earth&apos;s magnetic field. There are two permanent belts, an inner and an outer belt. A transient third belt may form during periods of high solar activity.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263, https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://doi.org/10.1029/2018JA025940</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>Van Allen belts</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015043 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015043">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ENVO_03000010"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The emission and acceleration of large numbers of energetic charged particles, mainly protons, from the Sun. Sources include solar flares and coronal mass ejections (CME).</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263, https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>SPE</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://three.jsc.nasa.gov/articles/Hu-SPEs.pdf</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>solar particle event</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015044 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015044">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ENVO_01000951"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The geomagnetosphere is the region of space surrounding Earth where the dominant magnetic field is the magnetic field of Earth, rather than the magnetic field of interplanetary space. The geomagnetosphere is formed by the interaction of the solar wind with Earth’s magnetic field.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263, https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://www.swpc.noaa.gov/phenomena/earths-magnetosphere</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>geomagnetosphere</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015045 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015045">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00002000"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A radiation exposure in which the delivery of energy to the target is considered to be &quot;high dose rate&quot; in a given context.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>The context here is determined by the type of radiation and application of the exposure.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <rdfs:label>high dose rate irradiation</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015046 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015046">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000016"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A radiation environment outside a commonly defined boundary marking the beginning of space (frequently the Karman line).</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>space radiation environment</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015047 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015047">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0090398"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Senescence caused by radiation and associated with the dismantling of a cell as a response to oxidative stress, e.g. high levels of reactive oxygen species, such as superoxide anions, hydrogen peroxide, and hydroxyl radicals.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>radiation-induced senescence</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015048 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015048">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000012"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A radiation detector which, when exposed to ionizing radiation, stores energy and when stimulated with light, releases the stored energy as luminescence, the intensity of which depends on the amount of radiation absorbed.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>OSLD</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>optically stimulated luminescence dosimeter</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015049 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015049">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000012"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A device which measures ionizing radiation dose by storing and then releasing the energy produced by the radiation. Energy is stored in the form of excited electrons. When the dosimeter is heated the electrons return to the ground state, resulting in the emission of light. The intensity of the emitted light directly corresponds to the radiation dose received, allowing for quantifiable measurements.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>TLD</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://www.landauer.com/blog/unveiling-science-thermoluminescent-dosimeters-key-radiation-safety-and-measurement</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>thermoluminescent dosimeter</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015050 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015050">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000011"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An active dosimeter in which ionizing radiation creates electron-hole pairs in a semiconductor which are collected by electrodes across which a voltage is applied. The resulting current is proportional to the energy deposited in the semiconductor by the incident radiation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <rdfs:label>semiconductor radiation detector</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015051 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015051">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000011"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An active dosimeter which estimates the amount of incident ionizing radiation by measuring the number of electrons liberated from the gas molecules by the radiation in a suitable housing.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>ion chamber</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>ionization chamber</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015052 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015052">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000073"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A senolytic is a type of drug or compound that selectively induces the death, removal or both of senescent cells—cells that have stopped dividing but often remain metabolically active, often secreting inflammatory and harmful molecules. These cells accumulate with age and contribute to aging-related diseases, chronic inflammation, and tissue dysfunction. </obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>Senolytics work by targeting specific survival pathways that senescent cells rely on, such as the BCL-2 family proteins, PI3K/AKT pathway, or p53-related mechanisms. By eliminating these cells, senolytics have shown potential in improving tissue function, delaying age-related diseases, and enhancing healthspan in preclinical studies.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263,https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9227-0670</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>See: Lelarge, V., Capelle, R., Oger, F. et al. Senolytics: from pharmacological inhibitors to immunotherapies, a promising future for patients’ treatment. npj Aging 10, 12 (2024). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41514-024-00138-4</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>senolytic</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010000 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010000">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000104"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000111>ALARA</obo:IAO_0000111>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>One of the measures to ensure ALARA is to minimize the time spent working around radioactive materials.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A principle of radiation protection philosophy that requires that exposures to ionizing radiation be kept as low as reasonably achievable, economic and social factors being taken into account. The protection from radiation exposure is ALARA when the expen</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>ALARA</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>ALARA</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>as low as reasonably achievable</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010001 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010001">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000182"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Attenuation in concrete or leadshielding is a common method of radiation protection on Earth, but is impractical in space due to mass constraints.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The reduction of radiation intensity upon passage of radiation through matter</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>radiation attenuation</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010002 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010002">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHMO_0002796"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Biodosimetry was used to measure the exposure of some victims of the Chernobyl nuclear accident.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Use of a biological response as an indicator of a dose of an effective agent (e.g., the extent of decline in peripheral blood lymphocytes of humans exposed to ionizing radiation can be used as an indicator of the absorbed dose to the whole body from that</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>biodosimetry</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010003 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010003">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005074"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>The bystander effect can be studied by identifying cells that were traversed by a charged particle.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The term used to describe the effects on cells in proximity to irradiated cells, but through which radiation has not passed. In experimental radiation biology, the bystander effect can be investigated by targeting individual cells, or by using doses low enough that the probability for a given cell to be hit by a particle is less than one.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>bystander effect</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010006 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010006">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000117"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Delta ray energies increase with the velocity of the incident particle.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Highly energetic electrons produced during inelastic collisions between ionizing radiation and atomic electrons. In a small proportion of collisions, the ejected electron receives a considerable amount of energy (i.e., &gt;1,000 eV), allowing it to travel a</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>delta electron</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>delta electron</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>delta ray</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010007 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010007">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010016"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>The average annual dose equivalent for crew members on the ISS is approximately 300 mSv.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The product of the absorbed dose (D) at a point and the quality factor (Q) at that point for the radiation type (i.e., H = DQ). The unit of H is J-kg^[-1] with the special name sievert (Sv)</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>dose equivalent</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010008 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010008">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00000119"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The increased risk from induced cancer due to exposure when the effects over an entire lifetime are accounted for. Individuals who would have died of cancer anyway but die early because of exposure are not included</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>excess lifetime risk of cancer</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010009 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010009">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00015001"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>The sample was irradiated with fast neutrons.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Neutrons of energies above a few hundred kiloelectron volts (keV)</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>fast neutron</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010010 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010010">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000109"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>The value of the fluence was approximately 1 particle per cm2.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The number of particles or photons per unit of cross-sectional area perpendicular to the direction of the particle (units of m^2 or cm^2) (i.e., particles-m^[-2] or particles-cm^[-2]).</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>fluence</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010011 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010011">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000109"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>The flux of cosmic ray muons per cm2 at the surface of the Earth is approximately 1.0.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The quotient of dN by dt, where dN is the increment of the particle number in the time interval dt. The unit for flux is reciprocal second (s^[-1].)</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>flux</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010012 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010012">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_33252"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00005003"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An atomic nucleus, fully stripped of its orbital electrons, containing 2 or more protons. Nuclei containing between 2 protons (helium) and 6 protons (carbon) are sometimes referred to as light ions.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>In RBO--needs def from NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>heavy ion</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010013 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010013">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000109"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Biological effects are a function of LET.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The energy deposited by an ionizing charged particle or photon in any medium per unit path length.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>LET</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>LET</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>linear energy transfer measurement</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010014 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010014">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_00010016"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Radiation protection standards take into account organ dose as well as whole body dose.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The mean absorbed radiation dose in a specified tissue or organ of the human body.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>organ dose</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010015 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010015">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001018"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Neutrons have a high quality factor compared to photons.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>In radiation science, a dimensionless factor developed for purposes of radiation protection and assessing health risks in general terms that accounts for the relative biological effectiveness of different radiations in producing stochastic effects and is used to relate absorbed</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>quality factor</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010016 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010016">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OBI_0000984"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>The samples received a dose of 1 Gy. (The use of the gray unit implies that &quot;dose&quot; refers to a measured quantity, typically absorbed dose or organ dose.)</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>The samples received a dose of 1 Sv. (The use of the sievert unit implies that &quot;dose&quot; refers to a der</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A measurement datum of the quantity of radiation absorbed by a substance or organism. The term &quot;radiation dose&quot; may for example refer to absorbed dose, organ dose, dose equivalent or equivalent dose, depending upon context and weighting factor(s).</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>radiation dose</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010017 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010017">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001018"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Radiation quality is often expressed in terms of the linear energy transfer of the ionising particles that deliver the absorbed dose. Rossi, Harald H. Specification of Radiation Quality. Radiation Research, vol. 10, no. 5, Radiation Research Society, 1959, pp. 522 31, https://doi.org/10.2307/3570787. But this is an over simplification.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Is a quality of radiation that reflects the fluence spectrum of charged and neutral particles within the material or medium where dose is being deposited.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf) and see https://melodi-online.eu/wp-content/uploads/2021/04/02-What-is-radiation-quality-Goodhead.pdf</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>radiation quality</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010018 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010018">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OBI_0001909"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Radiation weighting factors may change as more data on biological effects are obtained.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A factor used to allow for differences in the biological effectiveness between different radiations when calculating equivalent dose (H_T) (see equivalent dose). These factors are independent of the tissue or organ irradiated.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>radiation weighting factor</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010019 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010019">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000109"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Neutrons have been found to have a higher RBE than electrons.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Factor used to compare the biological effectiveness of absorbed doses from different types of ionizing radiation, determined experimentally. RBE is the ratio of the absorbed dose of a reference radiation to the absorbed dose of the radiation in question r</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>RBE</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>RBE</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>relative biological effectiveness</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010020 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RBO_010020">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001018"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>Tissue equivalent materials can be used to study radiation effects at depth in the human body.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Denotes a substance with absorbing and scattering properties for a given radiation that sufficiently match those of a particular biological tissue.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Jack Miller</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>NCRP Glossary (https://ncrponline.org/wp-content/themes/ncrp/PDFs/NCRP-Composite-Glossary.pdf)</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>tissue equivalence</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:Class>
    


